  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 336 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nWednesday will bring one final day of thunderstorms to portions of \nnorth Georgia before dry more autumn like air filters in behind it. \nTaking a look at the severe threat, warm temperatures and lingering \nSW flow from the day before should continue to provide us with \nplenty of instability out ahead of whatever linear system comes \ntogether across the northeastern portions of the CWA. There is still \na lot of uncertainty with regards to timing and strength, though \nmost models are in agreement that storms will likely continue into \nthe overnight hours before dissipating early Wednesday morning. \n\nAs the parent low pushes off the Atlantic coast, wedge like \nconditions set up as a strong high pressure in Northern New England \ngives us our first taste of fall weather. Temperatures are expected \nto fall into the low 80s on Wednesday and Thursday has us in the \nupper 70s with overnight lows in the 50s and 60s. Pumpkin spice \nseason has finally arrived. \n\nDry fall conditions are expected to continue through the weekend and \ninto the start of early next week as our rain chances fall to near \nzero. Should be a great weekend to get outside and enjoy the \nweather.",
